%% CASE Skylane crew scheduling replacement
Skylane Airlines is replacing its 20-year-old crew scheduling system. A vendor configures a commercial product under a fixed-price contract and a predictive plan, while two internal agile teams build the integrations and a mobile app for crew members. Go-live is planned for November, the airline's quietest month.

- A trial migration of the records of **1,800 crew members** found errors in **6%** of the records.
- The vendor's work will be accepted after a **four-week parallel run**, during which the old and new systems produce rosters side by side.
- The crew schedulers, the system's main users, are needed for testing but are fully occupied running daily operations.
- Vendor work status: BAC **$2,000,000**, PV **$1,400,000**, EV **$1,200,000**, AC **$1,500,000**.
@@ PR7 | Predictive
What should be done about the errors found in the trial migration?
A) Analyse the error types, fix the data at source with its owners and repeat the trial
B) Migrate the data as it is and let the crew schedulers correct the records after go-live
C) Accept the 6% error rate, since most of the crew records were migrated correctly
D) Ask the vendor to write a script that deletes all of the records that contain errors
= A
? Data quality problems are analysed and fixed at the source, and trial migrations are repeated until the agreed quality criteria are met. Errors in crew qualifications could create safety and legal problems.

@@ PR4 | Hybrid
How should the project obtain the crew schedulers' time for testing?
A) Agree with operations a rota and backfill that release schedulers at planned times
B) Ask the schedulers to test in the evenings after their normal operational shifts
C) Replace the schedulers with external testers who do not know the crew rules
D) Skip user testing entirely, since the parallel run will find any remaining problems anyway
= A
? Scarce business experts are planned as resources: their time is agreed with their managers, and backfill is arranged so that operations are not put at risk.

@@ PR9 | Predictive
What does the to-complete performance index for the vendor's work indicate?
A) A TCPI of 1.60, which is unrealistic, so a revised estimate at completion is needed
B) A TCPI of 0.63, which means the remaining work can easily be done within budget
C) A TCPI of 1.25, which is challenging but should be achievable with some extra effort from the vendor
D) A TCPI of 1.00, which means the vendor's work is exactly on budget at present
= A
? TCPI = (BAC − EV) / (BAC − AC) = 800,000 / 500,000 = 1.60. With a current CPI of 0.80, a 60% improvement in efficiency is not realistic, so the forecast should be revised.

@@ PR1 | Hybrid
What should determine whether the new system goes live after the parallel run?
A) Pre-agreed go/no-go criteria, such as roster accuracy and rule compliance
B) The planned go-live date, since November is the quietest month for the airline
C) The vendor's recommendation, since the vendor knows its own product best
D) The number of defects found during the parallel run, whatever their severity
= A
? Go/no-go decisions are based on criteria agreed in advance with the stakeholders, which makes the decision objective and transparent.
%% END CASE

@@ PR1 | Predictive
A chemical plant has a 21-day maintenance shutdown every four years. How should it be planned?
A) Months in advance, with an early scope freeze and a detailed shutdown schedule
B) In the last few weeks before the shutdown, once the condition of the equipment is known
C) Day by day during the shutdown, since the work found inside the equipment cannot be predicted
D) In the same way as normal daily maintenance, since the same maintenance team will do the work
= A
? Shutdowns are short, expensive and complex. They need long preparation, an early scope freeze and very detailed schedules.

